      "math_academy_id": "q-216190",
      "sequence_position": 11,
      "problem": "Let $x\\in R.$ Prove that if $0<x<3,$ then ${x}^{2}-6x+12>0.$\n\nThe theorem can be written as the implication \n\n$$\n{{field-1}}\\,⇒\\,{{field-2}}.\n$$\n\nThe {{field-3}}$\\,${{field-4}} of our implication is always {{field-5}}.\n\nTherefore, the theorem is \n{{field-6}}$\\,${{field-7}}.",
      "worked_solution": "We say that an implication has a *trivial proof* if the consequent is always true:\n\n$$\nP\\,⇒\\overset{\\text{Always true}}{\\overset{⏞}{Q}}\n$$\n\nIt does not matter whether the antecedent is true or false. \n\nWith that in mind, we proceed as follows:\n\nThe theorem can be written as the implication \n\n$$\n0<x<3\\,⇒\\,{x}^{2}-6x+12>0.\n$$\n\nNow, notice that for each $x\\in R,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}-6x+12 & =({x}^{2}-6x+9)+3 \\\\  & =(x-3)^{2}+3 \\\\  & \\ge 3 \\\\  & >0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have the following fact:\n\nThe consequent ${x}^{2}-6x+12>0$ of our implication is always true.\n\nFinally, we can make the following conclusion:\n\nTherefore, the theorem is trivially true.",
